{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792482543,"nonce":"YSnIYVlO9yscLfNhquNeyh7VjPY=","message":"The executable is zynode and the crate is zyntax_ts. Pinned Oxc parses and checks syntax/scopes once; frontend static typing builds shared TypedProgram nodes and canonical reference layouts. Primitives, annotated functions, recursion, branches, loops, numeric truthiness, source evaluation order, scalar-field records, aliases, borrowed returns and caller-owned objects execute natively. TypeScript assignment behavior is the default; an explicit checked policy rejects readonly weakening. Compile-time release analysis is enabled with Collector::None; no JavaScript transpilation, Node runtime or collector is used in the product.\n\nRayzor's ir/insert_free.rs and ir/drop_analysis.rs were inspected. The implementation reuses Zyntax drop_insert's alias, retention, returned-parameter and fresh-return facts rather than duplicating them in the frontend. HIR tests assert no callee free for transferred returns, exactly one caller free through borrowed aliases, idempotent reanalysis and frees on both early-return paths. A separate integration test verifies interpreter-first loading and correct actual Cranelift and installed LLVM execution. The promotion test exposed shared host scalar ABI issue 5dc1d16619f38cf21a29727a7d75c84dc46b569df4ab6bef856da0d5ac3bf1b7, fixed separately in 0e8b3256c8607b42ef662d880e1f173e948714e6.\n\nThe pinned TypeScript 6.0.2 and Node oracle covers 62 cases: 42 executable reference programs and 20 checking/policy cases. Interpreter and early-baseline controls produce 112 PASS and 12 XFAIL results: 98 TypeScript acceptance/output comparisons plus 14 opt-in policy checks pass; six tracked unsupported features appear once in each profile. Expected failures require full issue IDs; crashes, timeouts, wrong diagnostics and unexpected passes fail the harness. Node/TypeScript are optional development oracles, and Cargo tests run reference pins without either installed. The runner never invokes Cargo and checks that the native executable stays unchanged. Compiler-option matrices and upstream virtual files are supported; unsupported directives, relaxed checking and native multi-file projects are explicit SKIP results, never passes. CI runs live reference comparison and preserves its report.\n\nLocal ARM64 macOS validation: cargo test -p zyntax_ts --offline; cargo clippy -p zyntax_ts --all-targets --offline -- -D warnings; cargo fmt checks; seven Python harness tests; full pinned and live differential runs. LLVM 21 feature integration passed with LLVM_SYS_211_PREFIX=/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm cargo test -p zyntax_ts --features llvm-backend --test tiers --offline. No performance numbers or NUC benchmark claims are made.\n\nThe upstream adapter was exercised on eight downloaded conformance sources from Microsoft's TypeScript v6.0.2 conformance tree 9d28e54f5b0c7695ca2de6b1a15508dc35b0db98. All eight report UNSUPPORTED (legacy var, enums, ambient/function-value constructs), not compatibility passes. This is a smoke test of the driver, not an upstream-suite coverage result. Broader compatibility remains open under 6875fc2949f83d682861ecab814e0ff82c824f5990620b0d64ca0340ebb026e5.","files":null}]}